    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The way he describes them is pretty intense, though. He calls them \"savages\" and \"shapes\" and treats them like they aren't even real people, just part of the landscape. Was Conrad actually trying to be critical of that, or was he just using the language of his time to describe what he saw?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This ambiguity is central to the novel's enduring power. While Conrad undoubtedly utilized the dehumanizing language and tropes prevalent in the colonial era, his narrative forces the reader to question the very validity of those descriptions. By abstracting the Africans into \"shapes\" and suggesting their fundamental unknowability, he implicates the European observers in the very dehumanization they were perpetrating. Ultimately, the text critiques the racist lens through which the colonizers viewed the continent.",
      "source": "target",
